WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.590
Part 6 of nine: Learn with people affected by the

2
00:00:03.590 --> 00:00:03.790
work.

3
00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:08.340
The standards connected with this part are 12, 13.

4
00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:12.490
Here is the work this part helps you prepare: Plan

5
00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:16.490
meaningful, accessible input and explain how it will influence a

6
00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:16.790
decision

7
00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:21.380
In the curriculum's reference index, standard 12 is described as:

8
00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:24.340
Regular assessment of community assets and needs.

9
00:00:25.090 --> 00:00:29.650
In the curriculum's reference index, standard 13 is described as:

10
00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:34.110
Community partnership in design, implementation and evaluation.

11
00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:37.220
Ask before the decision is settled.

12
00:00:37.990 --> 00:00:40.830
If we want people to influence the notice, when should

13
00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:41.890
we ask for their input?

14
00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:46.640
Imagine a team revises the service-planning notice and asks for

15
00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:49.180
feedback only after the new version is approved and distributed.

16
00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:52.680
People may offer useful observations, but they have little opportunity

17
00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:56.420
to influence the original decision. Participation has arrived too late.

18
00:00:57.050 --> 00:01:01.390
Standards twelve and thirteen address understanding community assets and needs

19
00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:04.710
and partnering with communities in the design, implementation and evaluation

20
00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:08.170
of practice. For your individual work, the question is how

21
00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:10.810
you can support an appropriate process for learning with people

22
00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:12.170
affected, within your responsibilities.

23
00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:16.220
This lesson does not require you to organize a group

24
00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:20.020
or personally contact service recipients. You can complete every activity

25
00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:24.300
independently. Where engagement is needed, identify the established route and

26
00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:25.640
the people responsible for it.

27
00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:28.430
Clarify what can change.

28
00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:31.760
What do we need to be honest about before inviting

29
00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:32.380
that input?

30
00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:36.950
Before asking for input, describe the decision. What is open

31
00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:41.130
to change? What requirements or constraints must remain? How will

32
00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:44.390
input be considered, who will decide, and when will people

33
00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:45.250
hear what happened?

34
00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:48.700
For the notice, the team may be able to change

35
00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:52.440
the wording, layout and explanation of assistance, while a required

36
00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:56.260
time limit remains fixed. Say that clearly. Asking people to

37
00:01:56.260 --> 00:01:58.660
choose something that is not actually open can damage trust.

38
00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:03.080
A constrained decision can still benefit from meaningful participation when

39
00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:03.940
the boundaries are honest.

40
00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:09.030
Also ask whose experience is missing. People who readily respond

41
00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:11.750
to an online survey may not represent those who had

42
00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:15.830
difficulty accessing the original information. A single community member cannot

43
00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:18.050
be assumed to speak for every person with a disability,

44
00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:20.490
every language community or every family.

45
00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:22.960
Make participation usable.

46
00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:27.550
How do we keep the invitation itself from creating another

47
00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:27.890
barrier?

48
00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:33.840
Consider the format, language, communication support, timing, location or technology,

49
00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:38.060
and the effort required. An invitation that is inaccessible can

50
00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:41.480
reproduce the very problem the project hopes to address. Offer

51
00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:44.140
appropriate ways to participate through the approved engagement process.

52
00:02:44.990 --> 00:02:49.350
Respect a person's choice not to participate. Avoid making access

53
00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:53.130
to services appear dependent on providing feedback. Explain what information

54
00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:56.410
is requested, how it will be used and any limits

55
00:02:56.410 --> 00:03:01.890
on confidentiality. Follow applicable privacy and records requirements. If compensation

56
00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:05.650
or reimbursement is available, use the established rules; do not

57
00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:07.430
promise a payment you cannot authorize.

58
00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:12.680
Participation should make room for the person themselves, including self-advocates

59
00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:16.300
and their chosen supports. Do not automatically substitute the views

60
00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:20.200
of professionals or family members for the person's perspective. At

61
00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:23.060
the same time, different perspectives can reveal different parts of

62
00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:24.860
an experience when their roles are clear.

63
00:03:25.570 --> 00:03:27.850
Listen for assets as well as problems.

64
00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:31.480
When we listen, should we look only for problems?

65
00:03:32.230 --> 00:03:36.610
Community assessment includes strengths. Ask what already works, which communication

66
00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:40.570
approaches are trusted, and what people would want preserved. A

67
00:03:40.570 --> 00:03:44.410
redesign can unintentionally remove a feature that some people depend

68
00:03:44.410 --> 00:03:44.810
on.

69
00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:48.720
In our example, a clear telephone option may be valuable

70
00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:52.340
even if a new online response form is introduced. Improving

71
00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:55.760
one route does not necessarily justify removing another. Look for

72
00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:59.360
choices that support participation rather than assuming one channel will

73
00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:00.200
work for everyone.

74
00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:04.690
When feedback differs, describe the difference rather than forcing agreement.

75
00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:08.210
One person may prefer a brief notice, while another needs

76
00:04:08.210 --> 00:04:12.130
more explanation. A concise main message with accessible supporting detail

77
00:04:12.130 --> 00:04:15.730
might address both needs, but the proposed solution still needs

78
00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:15.910
checking.

79
00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:21.480
The assessment in standard twelve is recurring organizational work. It

80
00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:25.120
revisits community strengths and needs and uses that information in

81
00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:29.300
service planning and implementation, rather than treating one consultation as

82
00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:30.180
the complete picture.

83
00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:31.990
Close the loop.

84
00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:36.380
After people contribute, what does closing the loop involve?

85
00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:41.110
After input is considered, explain what changed, what did not

86
00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:45.190
change and why. Be specific without exposing personal information. If

87
00:04:45.190 --> 00:04:48.110
a suggestion cannot be adopted, acknowledge it and explain the

88
00:04:48.110 --> 00:04:51.470
relevant constraint or next step. Silence can leave people unsure

89
00:04:51.470 --> 00:04:52.870
whether their contribution mattered.

90
00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:59.180
Partnership continues into implementation and evaluation. People can help identify

91
00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:03.160
whether the revised notice actually improves understanding or whether a

92
00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:06.340
new barrier has appeared. That is different from using a

93
00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:08.500
consultation only to endorse a finished product.

94
00:05:09.270 --> 00:05:10.490
Plan your contribution.

95
00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:15.180
Let's make the participation plan concrete for someone working within

96
00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:16.020
a defined role.

97
00:05:16.730 --> 00:05:20.350
For your work example, write the decision that needs input,

98
00:05:20.950 --> 00:05:23.930
the perspectives that may be missing, an accessible way to

99
00:05:23.930 --> 00:05:27.250
obtain that input, and the role responsible for arranging it.

100
00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:29.970
Then describe how people would hear back.

101
00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:34.440
If you lack authority to initiate engagement, your action might

102
00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:37.560
be a recommendation to the responsible person. It can still

103
00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:41.880
be precise: ask for input before approval, identify the access

104
00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:45.180
considerations, and explain how the input could improve the decision.

105
00:05:45.950 --> 00:05:49.110
In the practice activity, choose an approach that gives people

106
00:05:49.110 --> 00:05:51.850
a real opportunity to influence the work while being honest

107
00:05:51.850 --> 00:05:54.830
about constraints. The aim is not simply to count participants.

108
00:05:55.650 --> 00:05:57.950
It is to improve the decision and the experience through

109
00:05:57.950 --> 00:05:58.690
meaningful involvement.

110
00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:02.860
Next, we will consider how to tell whether the change

111
00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:06.240
is helping. Your plan for participation will be part of

112
00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:06.720
that evidence.

113
00:06:07.890 --> 00:06:11.930
Let's work through the companion example: Invite input while it

114
00:06:11.930 --> 00:06:12.350
can matter

115
00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:16.160
Here is the situation. The team intends to ask people

116
00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:18.680
whether they like a revised notice after it is approved.

117
00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:22.190
What response would move this example forward responsibly?

118
00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:27.920
Recommend an accessible review before approval. Explain what can change,

119
00:06:27.940 --> 00:06:31.500
seek perspectives that may be missing, and plan to tell

120
00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:34.040
contributors how their input influenced the result.

121
00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:38.390
And here is why that response fits. Input can improve

122
00:06:38.390 --> 00:06:41.250
the decision when there is a real opportunity to act

123
00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:44.190
on it. Later evaluation is useful but serves a different

124
00:06:44.190 --> 00:06:44.530
purpose.

125
00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:48.800
Now make your own choice. A team wants input on

126
00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:51.640
a notice. Which approach offers the most meaningful participation?

127
00:06:53.310 --> 00:06:57.350
Option 1. Seek accessible input before approval, explain what can

128
00:06:57.350 --> 00:07:00.050
change and report back on how the input affected the

129
00:07:00.050 --> 00:07:00.350
decision.

130
00:07:01.040 --> 00:07:05.260
Option 2. Ask for endorsement after the finished notice is

131
00:07:05.260 --> 00:07:05.720
distributed.

132
00:07:06.370 --> 00:07:10.310
Option 3. Ask one person to represent everyone who may

133
00:07:10.310 --> 00:07:11.050
receive the notice.

134
00:07:12.220 --> 00:07:14.300
Pause the player now if you want to choose and

135
00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:16.800
explain your reasoning. You can use your own notes or

136
00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:19.460
the companion activity. When you're ready, resume and we'll work

137
00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:20.480
through all three responses.

138
00:07:21.270 --> 00:07:23.570
How should we think about option 1?

139
00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:27.620
This gives input a genuine purpose and closes the loop.

140
00:07:28.220 --> 00:07:31.600
Use the established engagement process and be honest about constraints.

141
00:07:32.410 --> 00:07:35.390
Option 1 is the supported choice in this activity.

142
00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:38.100
How should we think about option 2?

143
00:07:38.750 --> 00:07:41.790
People have little opportunity to shape a decision that is

144
00:07:41.790 --> 00:07:44.970
already complete. Later evaluation can help, but it does not

145
00:07:44.970 --> 00:07:46.170
replace earlier participation.

146
00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:49.200
How should we think about option 3?

147
00:07:49.810 --> 00:07:54.790
One perspective can be valuable without representing every experience. Identify

148
00:07:54.790 --> 00:07:58.050
whose perspectives are missing and appropriate ways to include them.

149
00:07:58.660 --> 00:08:01.860
You can choose another response and compare the reasoning.

150
00:08:02.630 --> 00:08:06.430
What decision needs input? Whose perspective is missing? Who can

151
00:08:06.430 --> 00:08:09.790
arrange accessible participation, and how will people hear what happened?

152
00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:14.180
The working note from this part is: Participation plan

153
00:08:14.890 --> 00:08:17.190
Pause the player if you want time to make that

154
00:08:17.190 --> 00:08:21.150
note. Keep it general, without names or private details. Thinking

155
00:08:21.150 --> 00:08:23.050
it through without writing is also fine.

156
00:08:23.660 --> 00:08:27.640
That completes part 6. You can return to any passage

157
00:08:27.640 --> 00:08:30.760
or take a break. The next part is Use evidence

158
00:08:30.760 --> 00:08:31.860
to improve access.
